  "Question": "The percent that \\(M\\) is greater than \\(N\\) is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\dfrac{100(M-N)}{M}\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac{100(M-N)}{N}\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac{M-N}{N}\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac{M-N}{M}\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac{100(M+N)}{N}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "For the usual meaning of percent greater, take \\(N>0\\) and \\(M>N\\). The increase from \\(N\\) to \\(M\\) is \\(M-N\\), and the reference quantity is \\(N\\). Therefore the percentage increase is\n\\[\n\\frac{M-N}{N}\\cdot100\\%.\n\\]\nThe numerical percent is consequently \\(\\boxed{\\frac{100(M-N)}{N}}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{B}\\). At \\(M=N>0\\), the formula gives \\(0\\%\\); a zero baseline \\(N=0\\) gives no defined percentage increase.",
